## ⚠️ The one rule that decides whether deploying works

**`index.html` must sit at the top level of your repository, not inside a
folder.**

This is the single most common reason a deploy returns "page doesn't exist".
Hosts look for `index.html` at the root. If your repository looks like the
left-hand column below, every host will return a 404:

```
✗ WRONG                              ✓ RIGHT
your-repo/                           your-repo/
└── hopes-door-portfolio/            ├── index.html
    ├── index.html                   ├── favicon.svg
    ├── favicon.svg                  ├── og-image.png
    └── ...                          └── ...
```

Check yours by opening the repository on github.com. **If the first thing you
see is a folder rather than `index.html`, that is the problem.**

### Two ways to fix it

**A. Move the files up (recommended).** In your local repo:

```bash
git mv hopes-door-portfolio/* .
git mv hopes-door-portfolio/.nojekyll .
git mv hopes-door-portfolio/.github .
rmdir hopes-door-portfolio
git add -A
git commit -m "Move site to repository root"
git push
```

Vercel redeploys on its own within about a minute.

**B. Or tell Vercel where to look.** In your Vercel project: **Settings →
General → Root Directory → Edit**, enter `hopes-door-portfolio`, save, then
**Deployments → ⋯ → Redeploy**.

