## If the deployed site still will not load

Work down this list in order. The first two cover almost every case.

**1. Is `index.html` at the root of the repository?**
Open the repo on github.com. You should see `index.html` in the file list
immediately, not a folder. If you see a folder, apply fix A or B at the top of
this file.

**2. Did the deploy actually finish?**
In Vercel, open **Deployments**. The newest one should say **Ready**. If it says
**Error**, click it and read the build log. If it says **Building** for more
than a couple of minutes, something is trying to run a build that does not
exist, which the included `vercel.json` prevents.

**3. Are you opening the right address?**
Vercel gives several. Use the one on the project overview marked as the
production domain. A preview address from an older commit can still 404 while
production is fine.

**4. Is the file named exactly `index.html`?**
Lowercase, no spaces, no `.txt` on the end. `Index.html` or `index.html.txt`
will 404. Some computers hide the real extension, so check on github.com rather
than in your file browser.

**5. Try the fastest possible sanity check.**
Go to <https://app.netlify.com/drop> and drag in the folder containing
`index.html`. It is live in about ten seconds with no configuration at all. If
it works there, the page is fine and the problem is a Vercel setting. If it
fails there too, the files did not upload correctly, so unzip a fresh copy and
try again.

